Name :
================
IP Source Guard

Why we did this
================
DHCP Snooping protects against Rogue DHCP Servers.
Dynamic ARP Inspection (DAI) protects against ARP Spoofing.
However, an attacker can still manually configure a valid IP address on their device.

Example:
Authorized User:
192.168.1.100

Attacker:
Manually configures 192.168.1.100

Even though DHCP Snooping and DAI are enabled, the attacker may still attempt to communicate using an unauthorized IP address.

Administrators needed a way to ensure a device uses only the IP address assigned to it.

To solve this problem, Cisco introduced IP Source Guard.

IP Source Guard uses the DHCP Snooping Binding Table to verify source IP addresses before allowing traffic.

What will happen
================
1. Device receives an IP address through DHCP.
2. DHCP Snooping creates an IP-MAC-Port binding.
3. IP Source Guard checks every packet arriving on the port.
4. Source IP is compared with the DHCP Binding Table.
5. If the IP matches:
   - Traffic is allowed.
6. If the IP does not match:
   - Traffic is dropped.
7. Unauthorized IP usage is prevented.

Example

DHCP Binding Table
------------------

192.168.1.100
MAC: 0011.2233.4455
Port: Fa0/1

If PC on Fa0/1 sends traffic using:

192.168.1.100
Result:
--------
Allowed

If PC on Fa0/1 sends traffic using:

192.168.1.200
Result:
--------
Dropped

Advantages and Disadvantages of this, hence what came next
==========================================================

Advantages
----------
1. Prevents IP address spoofing.
2. Works with DHCP Snooping.
3. Blocks unauthorized IP address usage.
4. Provides per-port validation.
5. Improves Layer 2 security.
6. Reduces impersonation attacks.
7. Complements DAI and DHCP Snooping.

Disadvantages
-------------
1. Requires DHCP Snooping.
2. Static IP devices require manual bindings.
3. Additional switch processing.
4. Incorrect bindings can block valid traffic.
5. More operational complexity.

Hence what came next
--------------------
Even after securing DHCP, ARP, and IP addresses, attackers could still connect unauthorized devices to switch ports.

Administrators wanted to track which device was connected to which switch port.

This led to:

Device Tracking

which maintains an IP-to-MAC-to-Port database and improves endpoint visibility.

One-Shot Configuration
======================

Step 1 - Enable DHCP Snooping

Switch(config)# ip dhcp snooping

Switch(config)# ip dhcp snooping vlan 10


Step 2 - Configure Trusted DHCP Port

Switch(config)# interface g0/1
Switch(config-if)# ip dhcp snooping trust


Step 3 - Enable IP Source Guard

Switch(config)# interface fa0/1
Switch(config-if)# ip verify source


(Optional - Verify Source Using MAC + IP)

Switch(config)# interface fa0/1
Switch(config-if)# ip verify source port-security

Verification
============

show ip verify source

show ip verify source interface fa0/1

show ip dhcp snooping binding

Expected Result
===============

Valid Source IP:
----------------
Allowed

Spoofed Source IP:
------------------
Dropped

Unauthorized IP Usage:
-----------------------
Blocked

DHCP Binding Table:
-------------------
Used for Validation

Security:
---------
Enhanced Protection Against IP Spoofing

Summary
=======

Problem:
Attackers could manually configure unauthorized IP addresses and impersonate legitimate devices.

Solution:
IP Source Guard verifies source IP addresses against the DHCP Snooping Binding Table.

Benefit:
Prevents IP Spoofing and unauthorized IP address usage.

Limitation:
Does not identify the physical device itself.

